# Reconciliator design

How a program of the Fearless family comes to hold a set of extensions, and makes the desk show the icons of those extensions and of the windows of that program.
The manager (see ControllersDesign) and the portable Fearless call the Reconciliator.

### The data

The 'identity' of the program calling `.reconcile` is the file name of `command`, without extension.
The 'Fearless family' is the set of the identities containing the text `earless`.
Two different versions, of the manager or of the portable Fearless, never share an identity.
```
ExtensionIcon: { .extension: FileExtension; .icon: FsPath }    // the .ico on Windows, the PNG on Linux and macOS
WindowIcon: { .windowClass: Str; .name: Str; .target: FsPath; .png: FsPath }  // Linux only
Refusal: { }    // what .ask needs; the caller does not look inside
Answer: { .taken: List[FileExtension]; .givenUp: List[FileExtension] }
Reconciliator: {
  mut .reconcile(sys: mut System, identity: Str, name: Str, command: FsPath, extensions: List[ExtensionIcon], taken: List[FileExtension], windows: List[WindowIcon], programIcon: FsPath, replaced: mut MF[FsPath, Void]): Opt[Refusal];
  mut .ask(sys: mut System, refusal: Refusal): Answer;
  mut .eradicateAll(sys: mut System): Void;      // deletes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family, with no check
  }
```
Each extension of `taken` is an extension of `extensions`.
`.reconcile` gives no description of its own to the type of an extension: the desk shows its own text for each extension.
`programIcon` is the icon of `command` itself: the PNG on Linux and macOS. Windows ignores `programIcon` and uses the icon inside `command`.
`name` is the name the desk shows for `command`: the portable Fearless passes `Fearless Portable`.
The 'setup' of an identity is every registry key and file `.reconcile` writes for that identity.
On Windows every `.reconcile` that passes its checks, and every `.eradicateAll`, deletes each ProgId `<j>.<ext without dot>` of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes`, with `<j>` in the Fearless family, that no setup on the desk names, with no call of `replaced`.
An identity 'releases' an extension when the setup of the identity stops naming the extension.
A `.reconcile` changes no setup when:
- the arguments are the arguments of the previous `.reconcile` of `identity`;
- each file the arguments name contains the bytes that file contained at the previous `.reconcile`;
- the desk gives each extension the types the desk gave that extension at the previous `.reconcile` (the types of the system MIME database on Linux, the 'declared type' on macOS).
A `.reconcile` given no extension, on a desk where `.reconcile` finds nothing of the Fearless family to delete, changes nothing and refreshes nothing.
Every other `.reconcile` that passes its checks ends with one refresh of the desk, also when no setup changed.
Every `.reconcile` and every `.eradicateAll` holds, from start to end, the lock of the file `fearlessAssociations.lock` in the folder where the desk keeps the data of the programs the user runs (`%LOCALAPPDATA%` on Windows, `$XDG_DATA_HOME` on Linux, `~/Library/Application Support` on macOS).
```
D, D'              the desk (see Desks in FolderShapesDesign) before and after a .reconcile
family(D)          the identities of the Fearless family on D (see The setup on each desk)
setup(D, i)        the extensions the setup of the identity i names on D
command(D, i)      the command of the setup of the identity i on D
holder(D, e)       the identity of the program D opens a file x<e> with, on a double click; that program 'holds' e
i, c, E            the argument identity, the argument command, the extensions of the argument extensions
```
After a `.reconcile` that refuses:
```
R0  D' = D
```
After a `.reconcile` that ends with no refusal, while no other program changes D:
```
R1  setup(D', i) = E  &&  (E != {}  =>  command(D', i) = c)
R2  E != {}  =>  family(D') = {i}
    E = {}  =>  family(D') = {}
R3  forall e in E. holder(D', e) = i
R4  forall j in family(D). command(D, j) != c  =>  replaced(command(D, j)) runs before .reconcile deletes the setup of j
```

### Taking an extension

`.reconcile` 'takes' each extension of `taken`, each extension of `extensions` that another program of the Fearless family holds, and each extension of `extensions` that the identity holds through a preference the user set in the desk (on Windows, through the key `UserChoice` or `UserChoiceLatest`), asking nothing:
- On Windows `.reconcile` deletes the keys `UserChoice` and `UserChoiceLatest` of each of those extensions. A key `UserChoice` or `UserChoiceLatest` that `.reconcile` deletes does not come back when the identity releases the extension.
- On Linux `.reconcile` removes, from every `*mimeapps.list` file the desk reads, each line, in any section, whose key is the type of the identity for one of those extensions (see Extensions on Linux).
- On macOS `.reconcile` writes into the preferences of LaunchServices a choice making the 'helper bundle' of the identity the program of the type of each of those extensions (see Extensions on macOS), in place of every other choice for that type, then restarts `lsd`, the service of macOS that opens files. macOS asks the user nothing.

### Refusals

The checks of `.reconcile` refuse in four cases:
- |family(D)| > 1;
- a program outside the Fearless family holds an extension of `extensions` that is not in `taken`;
- `.reconcile` can not take an extension it has to take;
- `.reconcile` can not write or delete a file, a folder or a setting of the desk that `.reconcile` has to change.

`.ask` shows the refusal to the user in dialogs of the Reconciliator, and returns when the user closes the last dialog.
The dialogs say what goes wrong and what the user can do: for each case above, in order,
- remove all the programs of family(D) but one;
- let the identity hold each such extension, or give it up;
- change the desk so that the identity can take each such extension, or give it up;
- give the identity the permission to change each such file, folder or setting.
`.ask` gives the extensions the user lets the identity hold as `.taken`, and the extensions the user gives up as `.givenUp`.

### The setup on each desk

The setup is the same for every version of every program of the Fearless family.
On Windows the setup of an identity is:
- the key `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\<identity>`, with `Capabilities` (the values `ApplicationName`, which is `name`, and `ApplicationIcon`, and the key `FileAssociations` mapping each extension to the ProgId of that extension) and `PreviousDefaults` (the previous default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>` for each extension, by extension);
- the value `<identity>` of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\RegisteredApplications`, naming `Software\<identity>\Capabilities`;
- for each extension, the ProgId `<identity>.<ext without dot>` in `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes`, with the icon of the extension as `DefaultIcon` and `"<command>" "%1"` as `shell\open\command`, named by the default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>` and by the `OpenWithProgids` of that key.
On Windows, when an identity releases an extension, the default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>` becomes the value `PreviousDefaults` keeps for that extension, or is deleted when `PreviousDefaults` keeps no value for that extension.
On Windows family(D) is the set of the value names of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\RegisteredApplications` and the `<j>` of the keys `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\<j>\Capabilities`, that are in the Fearless family.
On Windows the command of a setup is the command in `shell\open\command` of the ProgIds of that setup.
On Windows the identity of the ProgId `<j>.<ext without dot>` of an extension is `<j>`; the identity of every other ProgId is the whole ProgId.
On Linux the setup of an identity is `<identity>.desktop`, with `name` as `Name`, and the `.desktop` file of each window class in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/applications`, the MIME package of the identity (see Extensions on Linux), and the icons of the identity (see Icons on the desktop).
On Linux family(D) is the set of the file names, without extension and without a final `-` followed by hex digits, of the `.desktop` files in the `applications` folder of `$XDG_DATA_HOME` and of each folder of `$XDG_DATA_DIRS`, and of the files in the `mime/packages` folder of those same folders, that are in the Fearless family.
On Linux the command of a setup is the `TryExec` of `<identity>.desktop`.
On Linux the identity of a program is the file name of the `.desktop` file of that program without `.desktop`.
On macOS the setup of an identity is the 'helper bundle' of the identity, and family(D) is the set of the identities of the 'helper bundles' in `fearlessAssociations`.
On macOS the command of a setup is the command the script of the 'helper bundle' runs (see Extensions on macOS).
On macOS the identity of an application is the file name of that application without `.app`.

### Extensions on Linux

`.reconcile` gives each extension a type of its own, in the MIME package of the identity (the file `$XDG_DATA_HOME/mime/packages/<identity>.xml`, which only `.reconcile` writes):
```
<mime-type type="application/x-fearless-htm">
  <glob pattern="*.htm" weight="100"/>
  <sub-class-of type="text/html"/>
  <sub-class-of type="application/xhtml+xml"/>
  <icon name="<identity>-<hash>"/>
</mime-type>
```
- The MIME package gives each extension `<ext>` the glob `*<ext>` with weight 100, and one `sub-class-of` for each type the system MIME database gives to `*<ext>`.
- The `.desktop` file of the identity lists the types of the identity.
- `.fearless` keeps its type `application/x-fearless`. Every other extension gets `application/x-fearless-<ext without dot>`.

### Extensions on macOS

`.reconcile` declares each extension, `.fearless` included, in the 'helper bundle' of the identity.
The `Info.plist` of the application of each program of the Fearless family declares no type, and names `public.item` with `LSHandlerRank` `None`.
The 'helper bundle' of an identity is the application `~/Library/Application Support/fearlessAssociations/<identity>.app`, which only `.reconcile` writes: an `Info.plist`, `Contents/Resources/en.lproj/InfoPlist.strings` giving `name`, a script running `command` with the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened`, the icon of each extension and the icon of `command`.
The 'declared type' of an extension is the first type macOS gives for that extension that a bundle outside `fearlessAssociations` declares.
- An extension with no 'declared type' gets a type of its own: `<identity><ext>`, with `-` for each `_` and in lower case. The 'helper bundle' declares that type and opens that type as owner (`LSHandlerRank` `Owner`).
- An extension with a 'declared type' gets no new type: the 'helper bundle' opens the 'declared type' as alternate (`LSHandlerRank` `Alternate`). Holding such an extension makes `command` hold every extension of the 'declared type' (`.html` too when the extension is `.htm`).

### Icons on the desktop

On Linux `.reconcile` installs the `.icon` of each `ExtensionIcon` and the `.png` of each `WindowIcon` as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/mimetypes/<identity>-<hash>.png`, with `<hash>` the lower case hex digits of a hash of the image.
Every file named `<identity>-<hash>.png` in that `mimetypes` folder belongs to that identity: each `.reconcile` deletes the files of that identity that the `.reconcile` does not install.
`.reconcile` installs `programIcon` as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/<identity>.png`.
On Linux each window class of `windows` gets a `.desktop` file `<identity>-<hash>.desktop`, with `<hash>` the lower case hex digits of a hash of the window class, with `NoDisplay=true`, with `.name` as its name, `command` followed by `.target` as its command, the path of the installed `.png` as its icon and the window class as its `StartupWMClass`.
On macOS the `Info.plist` of the 'helper bundle' names an icon file for each type: `.reconcile` writes the icon of each extension as `Contents/Resources/<ext without dot>.icns`, an icns file containing the PNG scaled to 256 pixels. Finder shows the icon of a new extension at once. After the icon of an extension changes, Finder keeps showing the first icon of that extension until the icon store of macOS is rebuilt.

